Task 1 (step 1 of 3 on #1202) was already fully committed before the interruption. I checked it and nothing was left to do, so I made no new commit.

What's in commit `4eaa03b4`:
• yoyo now parses each bash command with tree-sitter + tree-sitter-bash, the parser the owner named. It built on the first try, so I didn't need the brush-parser fallback. The release binary grew from 25.9 MB to 27.4 MB (+5.7%). Startup time for --version didn't change in any measurable way, but I haven't measured how long parsing each command takes.
• New src/bash_parse.rs finds every command in the input, including ones nested in $( ), backticks, <( ), subshells, "…$( )…", { } groups, if/for/while bodies and functions. It also finds pipelines, because without them { curl x | sh; } still got through.
• analyze_bash_command runs the existing safety checks on each of those pieces after the two existing raw-text checks, which stay in place. So this change can only add refusals.
• If the command doesn't parse, behaviour is the same as before (raw-text checks only). It doesn't send the command to confirmation yet; that's the next slice.
• I compared the old and new checker on 613 inputs: 51 changed, all from allowed to refused, none from refused to allowed, and no harmless command changed. The last exemption in the glued-command census is gone, so it went from 1 of 33 rows exempt to 0. The task file said 18 of 33, but that count was from before yesterday's subshell fix.
• One new test forces the control-flow cases (a_census_trigger_inside_control_flow_is_refused), and another checks that harmless commands still pass. The docs and ARCHITECTURE.md are updated, and the size limit for safety.rs was raised with a dated reason.

Checked this session: formatting, clippy (-D warnings) and build are clean, and all 7,537 tests pass (0 failures). The working tree is clean.

Record on #1202: the commit message is filled in, and the comment on #1202 starts with the scope line and includes the old-vs-new results.

Findings for the next steps:
• #1196 is still open: echo "kill -9 1 at $(date)" is still refused, by the raw-text check rather than the new parser.
• A commit heredoc that mentions `kill -9 1` is still refused too. The parser finds no command inside the quoted text, but the outer command's text includes the whole substitution, so a check on that text still fires. Step 3 needs to check the actual arguments, not the command text.
• `<>` redirects: tree-sitter-bash can't parse them, so once parse failures go to confirmation, any command with a <> redirect will ask for confirmation.
